.TH KMONAD-DEVICE-MANAGER 1 "September 2026" "kmonad-device-manager 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kmonad-device-manager \- supervise one KMonad process per configured keyboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kmonad-device-manager
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager --doctor
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager --status
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager ps
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager devices
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager manager get
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager snapshot
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager events subscribe
.RI [ --after " EVENT_ID ] [ --server " SERVER_ID ] [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager identify start
.I DEVICE_ID
.RI [ --timeout " SECONDS ] [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager identify status
.I OPERATION_ID
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager identify cancel
.I OPERATION_ID
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager validate model
.I MODEL_FILE
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager validate file
.I KBD_FILE
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager apply
.I MODEL_FILE
.RI [ --name " NAME ] [ --id " CONFIGURATION_ID " --revision " REVISION ] [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager config
.RI { list " | create " MODEL_FILE --name " NAME " | update " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " MODEL_FILE " [ --name " NAME ] " | enable " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | disable " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | delete " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | adopt " EXTERNAL_CONFIGURATION_ID " [ --name " NAME ] }
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager --completion
.I SHELL
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager --version
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager
.RI { -h | --help }
.RI [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kmonad-device-manager
is a per-user Linux service that discovers KMonad configuration files, waits
for their input devices, and supervises one KMonad process for each available
keyboard. It is a supervisor, not a replacement for KMonad; configuration
syntax and remapping behavior are provided by KMonad.
.PP
The service requires the Linux evdev backend. On an unsupported platform
backend, it exits with a structured \fBunsupported_platform\fR error rather
than simulating device or process-management capabilities. macOS and Windows
are identified explicitly but remain unavailable until their separate native
backends are complete.
.PP
Configuration files are read transactionally. The manager validates each file
with \fBkmonad --dry-run\fR, refuses insecure world-writable paths, prevents
duplicate device claims, and retries failed launches with exponential backoff.
Configuration and device directories are watched with a polling fallback.
.PP
Every public invocation accepts \fB--json\fR. The option may appear before or
after the command selector. One-shot commands emit one JSON document. Service
mode emits JSON Lines to standard error. JSON errors contain an \fBerror\fR
object with \fBcode\fR and \fBmessage\fR fields.
.SH COMMAND INDEX
.TP
.B service
Run the foreground manager when no one-shot command is supplied.
.TP
.B --doctor
Check system readiness and configured keyboards.
.TP
.B --status
Read the authoritative manager status snapshot.
.TP
.B ps
Use the process-list-style status alias.
.TP
.B devices
List known keyboard-capable input interfaces.
.TP
.B manager
Read public manager metadata and capabilities.
.TP
.B snapshot
Read authoritative public manager state.
.TP
.B events
Stream ordered public manager events.
.TP
.B identify
Run, inspect, or cancel a keyboard keypress identification session.
.TP
.B validate
Preview a candidate without applying it.
.TP
.B --completion SHELL
Print an embedded completion definition for a supported shell.
.TP
.B --version
Show build version metadata.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show the complete command and option reference.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS SERVICE
.B kmonad-device-manager
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Service mode is selected when no one-shot command is present. It continuously
discovers \fB.kbd\fR files, waits for configured input devices, validates each
configuration, and supervises one KMonad process per available keyboard. It
runs until SIGINT or SIGTERM and is the invocation used by the supplied systemd
user unit.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Write operational logs as JSON Lines to standard error. Each object contains
\fBtime\fR, \fBevent\fR, \fBmessage\fR, and event-specific fields. This is
equivalent to setting \fBKMONAD_LOG_FORMAT=json\fR for this invocation. KMonad
output is wrapped as \fBkmonad_stdout\fR or \fBkmonad_stderr\fR with an
\fBoutput\fR field so raw child text cannot corrupt the JSON stream.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager
kmonad-device-manager --json
.EE
.SS DOCTOR
.B kmonad-device-manager --doctor
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Check Linux evdev platform support, KMonad availability, manager settings, membership in the \fBinput\fR and
\fBuinput\fR groups, the uinput kernel module and device access, configuration
security, configured input availability, KMonad dry-run parsing, and systemd
user-service state. A disconnected keyboard is a waiting condition rather than
a required setup failure. On an unsupported platform backend, doctor emits one
\fBplatform_unsupported\fR diagnostic with the detected platform/backend pair
without attempting Linux-specific checks.
The exit status is the number of failed required checks, capped at 255.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return \fBcommand\fR, \fBconfig_dir\fR, \fBhealthy\fR, \fBfailures\fR,
\fBwaiting\fR, and \fBchecks\fR. Each check is a Diagnostic with stable
\fBid\fR, \fBseverity\fR (\fBok\fR, \fBtemporary\fR, \fBwarning\fR, or
\fBerror\fR), \fBreason_code\fR, \fBsummary\fR, \fBremediation\fR, and
optional \fBresource\fR. JSON output never contains ANSI color escapes.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager --doctor
kmonad-device-manager --doctor --json
.EE
.SS STATUS
.B kmonad-device-manager --status
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Read the atomically published runtime snapshot and verify that its PID and
process start time identify the currently running manager. Report every known
configuration with state, detailed device availability and reason codes,
process health, process identity, retry timing, and failure context. Device
availability is \fBconnected\fR, \fBdisconnected\fR, \fBinaccessible\fR,
\fBunsupported\fR, or \fBconflicting\fR. Exit status 3 means the manager is not
running; stale status data is never presented as live state.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return the complete status document with manager identity, update time,
configuration directory, and configuration records. Errors are JSON objects on
standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager --status
kmonad-device-manager --status --json
.EE
.SS PS
.B kmonad-device-manager ps
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Process-list-style alias for \fB--status\fR. It reads and verifies the same
snapshot and has identical fields, output behavior, and exit statuses.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Emit the same status document as \fB--status --json\fR; errors are JSON
objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager ps
kmonad-device-manager ps --json
.EE
.SS DEVICES
.B kmonad-device-manager devices
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
List known keyboard-capable Linux input interfaces. This command does not
require the manager service to be running. It reports opaque manager device IDs,
available display metadata, availability, and stable reason codes, but never
prints platform input paths. Availability is \fBconnected\fR,
\fBdisconnected\fR, \fBinaccessible\fR, \fBunsupported\fR, or
\fBconflicting\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return a \fBdevices\fR array. Each item contains an opaque ID, display name,
vendor, product, serial when available, availability, identity stability,
configuration claim data, and a stable reason code. Errors are JSON objects on
standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager devices
kmonad-device-manager devices --json
.EE
.SS MANAGER
.B kmonad-device-manager manager get
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Read public metadata from the running manager without refreshing device
inventory or altering configuration state. The response identifies the API and
manager versions, KMonad version and compatibility, platform/backend versions, manager instance, current revision and
event cursor, public request and retention limits, capability availability, and
operational health. It never includes filesystem paths, device nodes, process
identifiers, or cgroup details.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return \fBapi_versions\fR, \fBmanager_version\fR, \fBserver_id\fR,
\fBplatform\fR, \fBbackend\fR, \fBstate_revision\fR,
\fBplatform_version\fR, \fBbackend_version\fR, \fBkmonad\fR,
\fBevent_cursor\fR, \fBlimits\fR, \fBcapabilities\fR, \fBlimitations\fR,
and \fBhealth\fR. Capabilities report per-device mapping and the supported
\fBdevice_file\fR input target; limitations list stable feature boundaries.
\fBkmonad\fR reports bounded startup version detection and current executable
availability without exposing its path.
Errors are JSON objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager manager get
kmonad-device-manager manager get --json
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a successful manager response, 1 if the manager is
unavailable or rejects the request, and 2 for invalid arguments.
.SS SNAPSHOT
.B kmonad-device-manager snapshot
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Read one coherent public-state view from the running manager. The snapshot
contains connected and known-disconnected devices, managed and read-only
external configurations, retained operations, public diagnostics, manager health, and a monotonic
state revision. It does not expose process IDs, filesystem paths, or device
nodes, and API/CLI failure does not affect reconciliation.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return \fBstate_revision\fR, \fBevent_cursor\fR, \fBdevices\fR,
\fBconfigurations\fR, \fBoperations\fR, \fBdiagnostics\fR, and \fBhealth\fR.
Each Diagnostic has a stable ID, severity, reason code, summary, remediation,
and optional resource. Health includes stable reason data, last progress time
when available, counters, and metrics/status availability. Errors are JSON
objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager snapshot
kmonad-device-manager snapshot --json
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a successful manager response, 1 if the manager is
unavailable or rejects the request, and 2 for invalid arguments.
.SS EVENTS
.B kmonad-device-manager events subscribe
.RI [ --after " EVENT_ID ] [ --server " SERVER_ID ] [ --json ]
.PP
Stream ordered public state-transition events from the running manager. With
\fB--after\fR, retained events newer than \fBEVENT_ID\fR are replayed before
live events. Pair a resumed cursor with the snapshot
\fBevent_cursor.server_id\fR using \fB--server\fR; a manager restart,
expired/future cursor, or slow client emits \fBmanager.resync_required\fR and
ends the stream. Fetch \fBsnapshot\fR and subscribe again. The stream cannot
block or control reconciliation.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I EVENT_ID
Optional non-negative event sequence from a prior stream; used with
\fB--after\fR.
.TP
.I SERVER_ID
Server ID from snapshot \fBevent_cursor\fR; used with \fB--server\fR when
resuming.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --after EVENT_ID
Replay retained events with IDs greater than this value before following live
events.
.TP
.B --server SERVER_ID
Require this snapshot \fBevent_cursor.server_id\fR when resuming with
\fB--after\fR.
.TP
.B --json
Write one public Event JSON object per line until the stream ends or a
resynchronization event arrives. Errors are JSON objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager events subscribe --json
kmonad-device-manager events subscribe --after 42 --server srv_0123 --json
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a normal stream close or
\fBmanager.resync_required\fR, 1 if the manager is unavailable or rejects the
request, and 2 for invalid arguments.
.SS IDENTIFY
.B kmonad-device-manager identify start
.I DEVICE_ID
.RI [ --timeout " SECONDS ] [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager identify status
.I OPERATION_ID
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager identify cancel
.I OPERATION_ID
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Ask the running manager to wait for a keypress from the opaque device ID
reported by \fBdevices\fR. To account for KMonad's input grab, the manager
pauses only the KMonad configuration using that device and restores it after
success, timeout, cancellation, or hotplug. Other configured keyboards continue
running. Only one identification session may run at once.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I DEVICE_ID
Opaque ID from \fBdevices\fR; required by \fBstart\fR.
.TP
.I OPERATION_ID
Opaque ID returned by \fBstart\fR; required by \fBstatus\fR and \fBcancel\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --timeout SECONDS
For \fBstart\fR only. Wait from 1 through 30 seconds; the default is 15
seconds.
.TP
.B --json
Return an \fBoperation\fR object with ID, state, target resource, timestamps,
reason code, and reason. Errors are JSON objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager identify start dev_0123 --timeout 10
kmonad-device-manager identify status op_0123 --json
kmonad-device-manager identify cancel op_0123
.EE
.PP
These commands exit 0 after a successful manager API response, even if the
returned operation has a terminal \fBfailed\fR, \fBcancelled\fR, or
\fBsucceeded\fR state. They exit 1 when the manager is unavailable or rejects
the request, and 2 for invalid command arguments.
.SS VALIDATE
.B kmonad-device-manager validate model
.I MODEL_FILE
.RI [ --json ]
.br
.B kmonad-device-manager validate file
.I KBD_FILE
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Ask the running manager to validate one candidate without applying it. The
\fBmodel\fR form reads a JSON object containing \fBdevice_id\fR and
\fBbehavior\fR; the manager renders the private Linux input target and
platform-owned output. Behavior must not declare \fBdefcfg\fR,
\fBdevice-file\fR, or an output sink. The \fBfile\fR form reads KMonad candidate
text directly. The manager checks size,
device availability and conflicts, validates an immutable runtime snapshot with
\fBkmonad --dry-run\fR, and removes the snapshot. It never writes the watched
configuration directory or changes a running mapping.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I MODEL_FILE
JSON model containing \fBdevice_id\fR and \fBbehavior\fR; required by
\fBmodel\fR.
.TP
.I KBD_FILE
KMonad candidate text; required by \fBfile\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return a \fBvalidation\fR object with outcome, reason code, reason, and
structured diagnostics. Model results include a \fBcandidate_digest\fR for the
exact submitted behavior. A rejected diagnostic can include an optional
\fBsubmitted_behavior\fR location with 1-based, half-open coordinates in that
behavior; an absent or unknown location is unmapped. Errors are JSON objects on
standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager validate model candidate.json --json
kmonad-device-manager validate file candidate.kbd
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a successful manager API response, including a
returned \fBrejected\fR or \fBblocked\fR validation result. It exits 1 when the
manager is unavailable or rejects the request, and 2 for invalid command
arguments.
.SS APPLY
.B kmonad-device-manager apply
.I MODEL_FILE
.RI [ --name " NAME ] [ --id " CONFIGURATION_ID " --revision " REVISION ] [ --json ]
.PP
Read a JSON managed model containing \fBdevice_id\fR and \fBbehavior\fR, then
ask the running manager to resolve the private input and platform-owned output,
revalidate it, write an immutable revision under its manager-owned state
directory, and activate only that mapping. The command
never writes the external watched configuration directory. A new configuration
requires \fB--name\fR; an update requires both \fB--id\fR and its current
\fB--revision\fR so a concurrent edit cannot be overwritten. Activation is
confirmed only after KMonad starts, joins its cgroup, and passes the manager
ownership and health check. If an update fails activation, the manager stops
the unconfirmed replacement, restores the prior immutable revision, and reports
\fBrolled_back\fR only when that known-good mapping passes its health check
again. A failed new configuration create removes its metadata rather than
leaving an unconfirmed mapping.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I MODEL_FILE
JSON managed model containing \fBdevice_id\fR and \fBbehavior\fR.
.TP
.I NAME
Display name required for a new configuration.
.TP
.I CONFIGURATION_ID
Opaque managed configuration ID to update.
.TP
.I REVISION
Current positive revision required for an update.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --name NAME
Set a new configuration's display name, or rename an existing configuration.
.TP
.B --id CONFIGURATION_ID
Select the existing managed configuration to update.
.TP
.B --revision REVISION
Require the current revision for an update; a stale revision is rejected.
.TP
.B --json
Return an \fBoperation\fR with state, reason code, reason, resource,
\fBconfiguration_revision\fR for the next update, and final validation result.
Errors are JSON objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager apply laptop.json --name 'Laptop keyboard' --json
kmonad-device-manager apply laptop.json --id cfg_0123 --revision 1 --json
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a successful manager API response, including a
terminal failed or rejected operation. It exits 1 when the manager is
unavailable or rejects the request, and 2 for invalid command arguments.
.SS CONFIG
.B kmonad-device-manager config
.RI { list " | create " MODEL_FILE --name " NAME " | update " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " MODEL_FILE " [ --name " NAME ] " | enable " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | disable " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | delete " CONFIGURATION_ID " REVISION " | adopt " EXTERNAL_CONFIGURATION_ID " [ --name " NAME ] }
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
\fBlist\fR inventories manager-owned and external configurations without
exposing platform paths. It reports the durable desired revision separately
from the last health-confirmed active revision, runtime/retry state, and the
most recent retained operation. External \fB.kbd\fR files are read-only unless
\fBadopt\fR can losslessly represent one canonical \fBdefcfg\fR device-file
input form. Adoption never rewrites the source file, requires unchanged source
bytes through managed activation, and hands supervision to the managed
configuration only after validation and activation confirmation. \fBcreate\fR
and \fBupdate\fR use the same
transactional validation, activation confirmation, and rollback behavior as
\fBapply\fR. \fBenable\fR preserves desired state so the mapping starts or
recovers automatically when its keyboard reconnects. \fBdisable\fR stops only
that configuration while retaining its immutable revision. \fBdelete\fR stops
that configuration and removes its manager-owned revisions. Each mutation
requires the current revision returned as \fBconfiguration_revision\fR by the
prior operation, preventing concurrent overwrites. A manager-owned revision
changed outside the manager is listed as failed and cannot be silently
overwritten.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I MODEL_FILE
JSON managed model containing \fBdevice_id\fR and \fBbehavior\fR; required by
\fBcreate\fR and \fBupdate\fR.
.TP
.I NAME
Display name required by \fBcreate\fR and optional for \fBupdate\fR.
.TP
.I CONFIGURATION_ID
Opaque ID returned by \fBcreate\fR; required by \fBupdate\fR, \fBenable\fR,
\fBdisable\fR, and \fBdelete\fR.
.TP
.I EXTERNAL_CONFIGURATION_ID
Opaque external ID returned by \fBlist\fR; required by \fBadopt\fR.
.TP
.I REVISION
Current positive configuration revision required by \fBupdate\fR, \fBenable\fR,
\fBdisable\fR, and \fBdelete\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --name NAME
Required by \fBcreate\fR and optional on \fBupdate\fR or \fBadopt\fR; sets the
managed display name.
.TP
.B --json
\fBlist\fR returns a \fBconfigurations\fR array with ownership, desired and
active revisions, runtime state (including \fBretry_at\fR when scheduled), and
\fBlast_operation\fR. Mutations return an \fBoperation\fR with state, reason
code, reason, resource, and \fBconfiguration_revision\fR. Errors are JSON
objects on standard error.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager config list --json
kmonad-device-manager config adopt cfg_0123 --name 'Imported keyboard' --json
kmonad-device-manager config create laptop.json --name 'Laptop keyboard' --json
kmonad-device-manager config update cfg_0123 1 laptop.json --json
kmonad-device-manager config enable cfg_0123 2 --json
kmonad-device-manager config disable cfg_0123 3 --json
kmonad-device-manager config delete cfg_0123 4 --json
.EE
.PP
The command exits 0 after a successful manager API response, including a
terminal \fBrejected\fR, \fBfailed\fR, or \fBrolled_back\fR operation. It exits
1 when the manager is unavailable or rejects the request, and 2 for invalid
command arguments.
.SS COMPLETION
.B kmonad-device-manager --completion
.I SHELL
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Print the completion definition embedded in the installed executable. This
keeps completions aligned with the installed version without requiring access
to the source tree.
.PP
Arguments:
.TP
.I SHELL
Required target shell. Supported values are \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, and
\fBfish\fR. Any other value is a usage error.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return \fBshell\fR and \fBcompletion\fR fields. The completion field contains
the full definition as a JSON string rather than executable shell syntax.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
source <(kmonad-device-manager --completion bash)
kmonad-device-manager --completion fish --json
.EE
.SS VERSION
.B kmonad-device-manager --version
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Print the version injected at build time. Release binaries report their release
version; development builds without an injected version report \fBdev\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B --json
Return \fBprogram\fR and \fBversion\fR fields.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager --version
kmonad-device-manager --version --json
.EE
.SS HELP
.B kmonad-device-manager
.RI { -h | --help }
.RI [ --json ]
.PP
Print the complete in-program command index, including every public invocation,
argument, option, output contract, example, and exit status. It carries the
same command information as this man page and the GitHub Wiki command
reference.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B -h, --help
Equivalent spellings that select the help command.
.TP
.B --json
Return structured help metadata with \fBprogram\fR, \fBsummary\fR,
\fBusage\fR, \fBcommands\fR, \fBexit_status\fR, and \fBfurther_help\fR. Each
command includes its arguments, options, JSON contract, and examples.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
kmonad-device-manager --help
kmonad-device-manager --help --json
.EE
.SH CONFIGURATION
Put files ending in \fB.kbd\fR in the configured directory. Each file should
contain an input declaration such as:
.PP
.EX
(defcfg
  input  (device-file "/dev/input/by-id/keyboard-event-kbd")
  output (uinput-sink "Managed keyboard")
)
.EE
.PP
The default directory is \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kmonad\fR, or
\fB$HOME/.config/kmonad\fR when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. The directory can
be changed with \fBKMONAD_CONFIG_DIR\fR or the installed environment file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B KMONAD_CONFIG_DIR
Directory containing KMonad \fB.kbd\fR files.
.TP
.B KMONAD_COMMAND
KMonad executable. Defaults to \fBkmonad\fR.
.TP
.B KMONAD_POLL_INTERVAL
Polling fallback interval in seconds. Defaults to 2.
.TP
.B KMONAD_STOP_TIMEOUT
Grace period before a stopping KMonad process is force-killed. Defaults to 5
seconds.
.TP
.B KMONAD_DRY_RUN_TIMEOUT
Maximum duration of KMonad configuration validation. Defaults to 30 seconds.
.TP
.B KMONAD_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT
How long an unhealthy supervised process may remain before it is stopped and
retried. Defaults to 60 seconds.
.TP
.B KMONAD_MAX_CONFIGS
.RS
Limits the number of configuration files considered in one run. The default
is 128. The provided systemd services allow 512 tasks for the manager and its
children; raise TasksMax if this limit is increased substantially.
.RE
.TP
.B KMONAD_MAX_CONFIG_BYTES
Maximum size of a \fB.kbd\fR file accepted by the manager in bytes. Defaults to
1048576 (1 MiB).
.TP
.B KMONAD_METRICS_ADDR
Optional TCP address, such as \fB127.0.0.1:9090\fR, for the Prometheus
compatible \fB/metrics\fR endpoint. Disabled by default.
.TP
.B KMONAD_CGROUP_ROOT
Optional delegated cgroup v2 directory. One child cgroup is created per
configuration and each KMonad process is placed in it.
.TP
.B KMONAD_PROCESS_MEMORY_MAX
Optional value written to the per-process cgroup's \fBmemory.max\fR file.
.TP
.B KMONAD_PROCESS_CPU_MAX
Optional value written to the per-process cgroup's \fBcpu.max\fR file.
.TP
.B KMONAD_LOG_FORMAT
Set to \fBjson\fR for structured JSON logs.
.TP
.B KMONAD_DOCTOR_COLOR
Set to \fBnever\fR to disable doctor output colors.
.SH STATUS STATES
.TP
.B discovered
The configuration was found but has not yet been validated.
.TP
.B validating
KMonad dry-run validation is in progress.
.TP
.B waiting
The configuration is valid but its input device is not connected or it is
waiting for reconciliation.
.TP
.B running
KMonad is running and its process identity and health checks pass.
.TP
.B failed
Validation or process startup failed; a retry is scheduled.
.TP
.B duplicate
Another configuration currently owns the same input device.
.TP
.B stopped
The process was intentionally stopped because the device, file, or ownership
changed.
.SH SYSTEMD
The recommended deployment is the per-user service:
.PP
.EX
systemctl --user enable --now kmonad-device-manager.service
journalctl --user -u kmonad-device-manager.service -f
.EE
.PP
The service uses process-group cleanup, resource limits, systemd readiness
notification, and a systemd watchdog. The manager sends \fBREADY=1\fR after
initialization and periodic \fBWATCHDOG=1\fR notifications when systemd
provides \fBNOTIFY_SOCKET\fR and \fBWATCHDOG_USEC\fR.
.SH INSTALLATION
The project provides an installer that does not require the Arch User
Repository. From a source checkout, run:
.PP
.EX
./install.sh
.EE
.PP
To install a downloaded prebuilt release binary while still installing the
service, completions, udev rules, and man page:
.PP
.EX
./install.sh --binary ./kmonad-device-manager
.EE
.PP
The installer places the user binary in \fB$HOME/.local/bin\fR and the man page
in \fB$HOME/.local/share/man/man1\fR. Arch packages place them in
\fB/usr/bin\fR and \fB/usr/share/man/man1\fR respectively.
.SH SECURITY AND RECOVERY
Only one manager instance is allowed per user. Status files are written to a
temporary file and atomically renamed. On an unclean exit, the next manager
instance uses stored PID start times, command lines, and configuration names
before terminating an orphaned process; PID reuse is rejected. Retry state is
restored from the last status file. Processes are placed in their own process
groups and inherit Linux parent-death cleanup.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/kmonad-device-manager/env
Optional user environment overrides.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/status.json
Atomic runtime status file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/kmonad-device-manager.service
Source-install user service.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/man/man1/kmonad-device-manager.1
Man page installed by the source installer.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful command or healthy status query.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid setup, dependency failure, or unreadable status.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage or settings, or an operation that requires the
unavailable Linux evdev backend.
.TP
.B 3
The manager is not running for a status query.
.TP
.B 127
Service mode could not find the configured KMonad executable.
